{
 "result": "Built maze.py: an end-to-end shortest-path solver for '#'/'.'/S/E grids. It reads all of stdin, splits rows (handling CRLF, ragged/short rows, missing trailing newline), locates S and E, and runs a cache-friendly BFS over a flat int32 distance array + ring-free int32 queue with 4-neighbour expansion and inlined bounds checks; prints the distance to E or -1 (also for missing S/E, empty input, or allocation failure).\n\nFor speed it embeds a C implementation (C_SRC) which it compiles once with cc/gcc/clang -O2 into a content-hashed binary (.maze_bin_<sha1>) next to the script (compile output directed via TMPDIR to the working folder), then execs it on every later call; if no compiler is available it falls back to a pure-Python collections.deque BFS, so behaviour is identical either way.\n\nEvidence: assay.py (registered as the fitness command) validates ~400 random small/medium grids, including unreachable targets, missing E, CRLF line endings and no trailing newline, against an independent Python BFS reference, and requires the 2000x2000 seeded maze answer (5198) to match a stored expectation and be stable across runs. The assay runs 8 timed end-to-end process invocations on that big maze (~0.5 s each, well above startup noise) and prints runs/second: median baseline 16.5446, noise 1.8%, exit 0. Manual check confirms maze.py prints 5198, matching .tmp/maze_big.ans.",
 "calls": 16,
